      en: "The Colonnaded Street in Petra, also called the Via Sacra, stretches about 400 meters through the heart of the ancient Nabataean city. Built in the 1st century AD under kings Aretas IV and Malichus II, it was later expanded by the Romans after the annexation in 106 AD. The street was lined with over 100 columns, many still standing. It served as the central axis for processions and trade. The eastern end terminates at the Temenos Gate, while the western leads to the Great Temple and Qasr al-Bint. The width of about 6 meters allowed pedestrian and caravan traffic. Excavations have revealed remains of shops and administrative buildings.",
